Hand-rolled loops are error-prone: off-by-one bounds, duplicated logic, and subtle bugs. The **Standard Template Library (STL)** replaces them with battle-tested algorithms in `<algorithm>` and `<numeric>`.

### sort

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> nums = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
std::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());   // 1 1 3 4 5
```

`sort` takes two **iterators**: `begin()` is the first element and `end()` is one past the last. It sorts in place with O(n log n) worst-case complexity — far better than the O(n²) bubble sort you might write by hand.

### find

```cpp
auto it = std::find(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 4);
if (it != nums.end()) {
    std::cout << "found at index " << (it - nums.begin());
}
```

`find` returns an iterator to the first match, or `end()` if nothing matched. Comparing the result against `end()` is the standard "did I find it?" idiom.

### accumulate, min_element, max_element

```cpp
#include <numeric>

int total = std::accumulate(nums.begin(), nums.end(), 0);
int lo = *std::min_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());
int hi = *std::max_element(nums.begin(), nums.end());
```

`accumulate` starts with the third argument (the initial value `0`) and folds the range into it — that's a sum. `min_element` and `max_element` return iterators, so dereference with `*` to read the value.

### Lambdas with sort

For custom ordering, pass a **lambda** as a third argument:

```cpp
std::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end(),
          [](int a, int b) { return a > b; });   // descending
```

### Why STL over raw loops

STL algorithms are correct (tested by millions of programs), self-documenting (the name says what they do), and safe (no manual bounds). When a loop is just "do this to every element", the algorithm is faster to write and faster to read — and leaves less room for off-by-one mistakes.

codeSnippets.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
    std::sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    for (int n : nums) {
        std::cout << n << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
